<Base title="Volunteering and ANU+ — Career Central">
  <div class="prose">
  <h1>Volunteering, and the ANU+ award</h1>

  <h2>Skilled vs unskilled volunteering</h2>
  <p>
    Both count, and both matter — they just demonstrate different things to a
    future employer.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Unskilled (general) volunteering</strong> — event support, working
      bees, op-shop shifts, food bank sorting. No specific qualification
      needed; shows up reliably, works well in a team, contributes without
      being asked twice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skilled volunteering</strong> — using what you're actually
      studying: tutoring, treasurer or committee roles that use financial or
      legal literacy, building a website for a not-for-profit, peer mentoring
      in your discipline. This is the kind that reads most directly onto a
      resume for a related job, because it's the same skill, just unpaid.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Neither is "better" — pick what fits the time you actually have, and what
    you want to be able to talk about in an interview later.
  </p>

  <h2>The ANU+ award</h2>
  <p>
    ANU+ is ANU's structured, non-academic program that recognises volunteering
    through guided reflection, and marks the completed award on your academic
    transcript. It's open to all enrolled undergrad and postgrad students, and
    it's run out of the ANU McCusker Institute.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Join it through Career Central</strong> — log in there and join
      the undergraduate or postgraduate ANU+ pathway before you start counting
      hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Internal volunteering</strong> (ANUSA clubs & societies, ANU
      services and teams) has to be registered and approved through the ANU+
      team first — register the role before you start it, not after.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>External volunteering</strong> — unpaid work with not-for-profits
      or co-ops outside ANU — also counts, generally with lighter registration
      overhead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Training hours don't count.</strong> Onboarding and induction time
      is preparation, not the volunteering itself — only log hours once you're
      actually doing the role.
    </li>
    <li>
      A supervisor signs off every 10 hours. Get that sign-off as you go —
      trying to reconstruct months of hours at the end of the year is much
      harder than it needs to be.
    </li>
    <li>
      Completing the program is recognised with an official award on your
      transcript, and along the way it's meant to build resilience,
      leadership, and cultural awareness through the reflection component, not
      just the hours themselves.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not sure whether a role you're considering would count, or how to get it
    registered with ANU+? That's the kind of question worth{" "}
    <a href="/coaching">booking a query</a> about, or raising directly with the ANU+
    team.
  </p>
  <p>
    <small>
      Sources: ANU's Volunteer Fair coverage and the ANU+ procedure document
      published by the University.
    </small>
  </p>
  </div>
</Base>
